# Swiss UI + Integration Rule

Before proposing or writing frontend code, first model the existing stack and system:
- Identify framework/styling stack and component architecture.
- Reuse existing naming, patterns, and folder conventions.
- Centralize tokens and avoid one-off styles.
- Preserve accessibility and responsive behavior.

If scope is ambiguous, ask focused questions first:
- Is this a redesign, refactor-to-system, or new feature?
- Which files/components are in scope?

## Required Design Language (Swiss / International Typographic Style)
- Objective, content-first communication; minimal ornament.
- Grid-led, asymmetric layout; strict left alignment.
- Typography as structure (Inter/Helvetica-like; strong hierarchy, uppercase labels).
- Active negative space; visible structure and separators.
- Flat visual depth via subtle pattern texture (grid/dots/diagonal/noise), not shadows.

## Tokens (default)
- Background `#FFFFFF`, Foreground `#000000`, Muted `#F2F2F2`, Accent `#FF3000`, Border `#000000`.
- Rectangular geometry (`radius: 0`), visible borders (`2px+`), no drop shadows.
- Accent red is functional (CTA/highlight/warning), used sparingly.

## Components and Interaction
- Buttons/cards/inputs are rectangular and high-contrast.
- Hover/focus states are explicit (inversion/red accents), not subtle fades.
- Motion is short, mechanical, purposeful (`150-200ms`), respect reduced motion.
- Maintain AA contrast and keyboard accessibility.

## Implementation Standards
- Prefer reusable primitives and composable components.
- Minimize duplication; extract shared tokens/utilities.
- Explain major design/architecture choices briefly while implementing.
- Leave code cleaner than found.
